00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:11.815
It was amazing.

00:06:11.815 --> 00:06:12.605
[HEAVENLY CHOIR]

00:06:12.605 --> 00:06:13.730
STEWART REICHLING: Awesome.

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:15.290
So it sounds like
some people have

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:17.570
started to get used to the
idea of Cloud Functions.

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:20.270
But just for those who haven't
really had a chance yet,

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:22.280
Cloud Functions is
one of our serverless

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:23.960
compute products from Google.

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:26.070
Now, "serverless"--
what does that mean?

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:29.166
That means that you give us your
code, and we run it for you.

00:06:29.166 --> 00:06:31.040
You don't have to think
about infrastructure.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:32.390
It scales automatically.

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:34.190
You only pay for what you use.

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:37.010
And what we really focus on
is having a very idiomatic

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:38.160
developer experience.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:40.460
So making sure that, if
you're a Go developer,

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:43.280
it looks, it feels,
it smells like Go.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:45.680
It's something that's
familiar to you.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:47.180
And so the idea
with Cloud Functions

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:49.220
is you give us that
little bit of code,

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:51.426
it runs in response to
an event, and we handle

00:06:51.426 --> 00:06:52.550
all the hard stuff for you.

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:54.008
TYLER BUI-PALSULICH:
One way I like

00:06:54.008 --> 00:06:57.080
to explain it is, kind of,
in the spectrum of ways

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:01.709
you can run your code is you
come up with some application,

00:07:01.709 --> 00:07:03.250
and you're running
it on your laptop.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:05.480
And you say, hey, look at this.

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:06.620
This is so cool.

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:08.060
Then you say, all
right, maybe I'm

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:09.260
going to put this on a server.

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:11.010
And I'm going to stick
that in the closet,

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:14.420
and I'm going to keep the power
on and the air conditioning on.

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:17.420
And then once that gets a
little bit too cumbersome,

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:21.950
maybe you put a VM on-premise,
or maybe some cloud provider,

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:23.580
and that gets too cumbersome.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:26.000
So then you have some
automatically scaling

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:29.379
application service,
say, Google's App Engine.

00:07:29.379 --> 00:07:30.920
And if that's a
little bit overweight

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:34.790
for what you are working
on, then Functions

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:36.440
can be the next
step, where, instead

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:40.130
of writing an entire application
for what you need to do, maybe

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:44.840
it's just a single function
that resizes images, or does

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:47.360
some small concrete
task that doesn't

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:50.150
require an entire application
to be running somewhere.

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:51.980
AJA HAMMERLY: So that
sounds super cool.

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:54.077
I like the imagery
resizing example.

00:07:54.077 --> 00:07:55.910
Can you give us some
other examples of stuff

00:07:55.910 --> 00:07:57.305
that does really well
on Cloud Functions?

00:07:57.305 --> 00:07:58.310
STEWART REICHLING: Sure.

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:00.620
I've got some examples
of use cases of things

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:01.580
that we've seen.

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:04.460
And all of these, kind
of, fall within the bucket

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:06.170
of event-driven computing.

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:09.320
So something happens, you
want something else to happen

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:12.560
in response, a sort of
programmable computing

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:14.520
layer when an event happens.

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:17.630
So for example, imagine you
have a Cron Job right now

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:21.470
that's running on your machine,
and it runs once a day.

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:24.140
Well, does it really make
sense for that machine

00:08:24.140 --> 00:08:26.120
to be the thing where
your code lives?

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:27.710
And if that machine
goes down, you

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:30.000
have to maintain it and
figure out what's happening.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.210
So one example use
case is you have

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:33.830
a product like Cloud Scheduler.

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:36.890
Cloud Scheduler sends an
event to Cloud Functions

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:40.220
on a schedule every day, for
example, and then you run code.

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:43.429
It might be something that goes
and looks through your database

00:08:43.429 --> 00:08:45.470
and cleans up stale files.

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:48.830
It might be something that
does a data transformation

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:50.400
on a regular schedule.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:51.390
So that's one example.

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:53.210
Another very common
use case I see

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:55.880
is gluing together
either different clouds,

00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:58.550
or even within Google
Cloud, different services.

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:01.220
So you might have
events coming off

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:05.010
of a message bus using
Pub/Sub, for example,

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:08.960
and you want to move that
data from that message bus

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:12.350
into a more
persistent data store.

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:14.180
So a Cloud Function
is a very easy way

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:15.770
to say, OK, I get that message.

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:18.140
Maybe I do a very small
transformation on it,

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:22.065
and now I load it into my cloud
SQL or into my Cloud Firestore.

00:09:22.065 --> 00:09:22.940
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:26.000
So we're here to talk
about Go Cloud Functions.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.034
What's special about
Go Cloud Functions,

00:09:28.034 --> 00:09:30.450
other than, I mean, I can write
Go, which is kind of cool?

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:32.220
STEWART REICHLING: Yeah,
so, as I mentioned earlier,

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:34.250
one of the things that
we really try to focus on

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:37.382
is having a very idiomatic
developer experience.

00:09:37.382 --> 00:09:39.590
So that means if you write
Node, if you write Python,

00:09:39.590 --> 00:09:41.954
if you like Go, when
you touch the product

00:09:41.954 --> 00:09:43.370
and you use the
product, it should

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:45.370
feel very familiar to you.

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:47.340
And to help us
achieve that goal,

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:50.960
I actually lean very heavily
on our developer programs

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:53.990
engineers like Tyler because
Tyler is a Go expert.

00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:56.240
I know these languages
reasonably well,

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:57.950
but I really look to
someone like Tyler

00:09:57.950 --> 00:10:00.800
to bring his expertise when
we design this product so

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:03.650
that, as someone who's been
working on Go for a long time,

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:04.884
it should feel very familiar.

00:10:04.884 --> 00:10:06.800
Tyler, you want to talk
a little bit about why

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:07.790
we think this is special?

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:09.164
TYLER BUI-PALSULICH:
Yeah, so one

00:10:09.164 --> 00:10:10.760
of the important
things for me is

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:13.310
that writing your
application should not

00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:16.680
be specific to where you
are running that code.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:21.830
So for Cloud Functions, in
particular for HTTP functions,

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:28.310
a Go HTTP Cloud Function
is an HTTP HandlerFunc,

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:31.880
so you don't need a
third-party import.

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:34.440
You don't need anything special.

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:36.380
It's just all of the
handlers that you've

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:39.290
written in the past
can be run directly

00:10:39.290 --> 00:10:41.354
on Cloud Functions with
minimal modification.

00:10:41.354 --> 00:10:42.770
STEWART REICHLING:
Just for people

00:10:42.770 --> 00:10:44.728
who aren't completely
familiar with the product

00:10:44.728 --> 00:10:46.700
yet, with Cloud
Functions we, basically,

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:49.340
provide two ways of
invoking your function.

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:53.330
One is via an HTTP web hook.

00:10:53.330 --> 00:10:54.590
That's the classic use case.

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:57.230
We give you HTTP signature.

00:10:57.230 --> 00:11:00.480
We let you interact directly
with the HTTP object.

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:02.090
And so, in order
to implement that,

00:11:02.090 --> 00:11:06.440
we used Go's idiomatic
standard HTTP HandlerFunc.

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:09.200
You send a request to
the URL that we give you,

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:11.340
and your code runs
in response to that.

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:13.810
The other way that you
can run Cloud Functions

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:16.030
is background
functions, or functions

00:11:16.030 --> 00:11:19.270
that are triggered by events
emitted by Google Cloud

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:23.059
infrastructure, so things
like Pub/Sub, Google Cloud

00:11:23.059 --> 00:11:24.100
Storage, Cloud Firestore.

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:25.330
MARK MANDEL: And
does the interface

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:26.860
for that look slightly
different, I assume?

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:28.943
TYLER BUI-PALSULICH: It
is a little bit different.

00:11:28.943 --> 00:11:31.570
So we tried to make
it a little bit easier

00:11:31.570 --> 00:11:34.030
to interact with those
different event types,

00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:36.850
rather than direct HTTP.

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:39.430
And so the hope
is that you still

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:44.020
aren't writing code that does
too much heavy lifting, where

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:47.720
you have to parse exactly
what's in every single request.

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:50.970
And so what those look
like is it's a function.

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:54.230
It takes a standard
Go context object,

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:58.060
which carries information,
like request-scoped values,

00:11:58.060 --> 00:11:59.870
deadlines, and so on.

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:03.670
And you have one more
argument, that is event type

00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:05.090
that you care about.

00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:08.650
And so our runtime
unmarshalls what

00:12:08.650 --> 00:12:14.260
comes in as json into the
event type that you provide.

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:16.310
And then the function
returns an error,

00:12:16.310 --> 00:12:19.780
which indicates, basically,
was this a success or not?

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:21.820
And then, based on
that return value,

00:12:21.820 --> 00:12:25.140
you can optionally enable
retrials for your function.

00:12:25.140 --> 00:12:26.140
STEWART REICHLING: Yeah.

00:12:26.140 --> 00:12:27.700
And so when we say "idiomatic"--

00:12:27.700 --> 00:12:29.200
SPEAKER: What did
you just call me?

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:29.770
STEWART REICHLING:
--a lot of people

00:12:29.770 --> 00:12:31.870
say, what does
that actually mean?

00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:34.780
The goal here is that
we want to minimize

00:12:34.780 --> 00:12:37.450
the amount of new stuff
that you have to learn

00:12:37.450 --> 00:12:40.480
and the cognitive overhead when
you're developing something.

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:43.660
We want to make it super
easy and super quick, as a Go

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:45.760
developer, for you to be
able to write a function,

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:49.027
not learn anything new, deploy
it, and start seeing events

00:12:49.027 --> 00:12:49.860
come in immediately.

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:52.540
MARK MANDEL: So I'm guessing
that also translates to, like,

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:53.939
local development experience?

00:12:53.939 --> 00:12:55.480
If it's just a
standard HTTP handler,

00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:56.830
I can just use standard stuff.

00:12:56.830 --> 00:12:57.280
Is that right?

00:12:57.280 --> 00:12:58.738
TYLER BUI-PALSULICH:
Yeah, exactly.

00:12:58.738 --> 00:13:01.570
So the way that's
usually set up is

00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:03.790
having a command
subdirectory, which

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:07.130
is a common pattern for
Go projects, in general.

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:10.300
And so you create that
directory, put in a package

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:16.240
main with an HTTP server in
there, listen for the functions

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:18.040
that you care about, and run it.

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:21.580
And I think that might
provide some friction

00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:25.220
around connecting,
say, Pub/Sub where

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:28.980
you're using the real Pub/Sub
service to run your functions.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:31.720
But for HTTP, it
works really well.

00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:34.360
And testing is also really nice.

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:36.820
One of the other things that
we support with this runtime

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:41.800
is that Go 1.11 introduces
this concept of go.mod,

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:45.750
which allows you to specify your
dependencies in a go.mod file.

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:49.180
It supports Go 1.11
modules functionality.

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:52.427
And that, basically, brings
to the language behavior

00:13:52.427 --> 00:13:54.010
that is very familiar
to someone who's

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:56.710
been using Node
with package.json

00:13:56.710 --> 00:13:59.470
or Python with
requirements text.

00:13:59.470 --> 00:14:02.740
And so, basically, this
allows you to just write

00:14:02.740 --> 00:14:04.060
your dependencies in a file.

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:05.726
You don't have to
worry about vendoring.

00:14:05.726 --> 00:14:08.920
You don't have to worry about
managing the actual source

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:10.330
code you deploy.

00:14:10.330 --> 00:14:13.180
Our infrastructure
inspects that go.mod,

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:14.680
and it will install
the dependencies

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:17.036
and resolve those for
you in your container.

00:14:17.036 --> 00:14:18.910
So we feel that that's
a very easy way for us

00:14:18.910 --> 00:14:21.629
to show that we're
incorporating new Go language

00:14:21.629 --> 00:14:23.920
features, staying up to date
with what the community is

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:27.070
doing, and also providing a
very simple developer experience

00:14:27.070 --> 00:14:28.300
that is new to the language.

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:30.070
But we feel pretty
good about how

00:14:30.070 --> 00:14:33.070
that's going to find a bigger
following in the future.

00:14:33.070 --> 00:14:36.430
AJA HAMMERLY: So Google has
a lot of serverless products.

00:14:36.430 --> 00:14:38.530
How exactly is Cloud
Functions different than,

00:14:38.530 --> 00:14:39.236
say, App Engine?

00:14:39.236 --> 00:14:41.110
When would I want to
choose one or the other?

00:14:41.110 --> 00:14:42.110
STEWART REICHLING: Sure.

00:14:42.110 --> 00:14:44.320
So there are a lot of
similarities between the two

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:45.050
products.

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:47.230
I talked earlier
about not having

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:50.470
to manage infrastructure,
really paying for the resources

00:14:50.470 --> 00:14:53.506
that you're using, scaling
up and down to zero.

00:14:53.506 --> 00:14:55.630
So if you're getting no
traffic, you're not paying.

00:14:55.630 --> 00:14:57.970
But there are also some
pretty key differences.

00:14:57.970 --> 00:14:59.920
And the way I like to
think about it is you

00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:02.690
want to use the right
tool for the right job.

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:05.830
So App Engine is really
well suited for web apps.

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:09.400
You're setting up a store that
has multiple different routes,

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:11.020
and you want to
make sure that when

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:12.561
you get a request
to a certain route,

00:15:12.561 --> 00:15:15.100
you render a certain
page back to your user.

00:15:15.100 --> 00:15:19.310
Cloud Functions is really all
about the event-based use case,

00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:22.580
so you're not, necessarily,
setting up multiple routes.

00:15:22.580 --> 00:15:24.460
You are writing
single-purpose code

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:26.740
that scales automatically
and responds

00:15:26.740 --> 00:15:28.720
to the incoming event load.

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:30.720
And we try to make
that clear, also,

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:32.470
through the feature
sets that are provided

00:15:32.470 --> 00:15:33.636
with the different products.

00:15:33.636 --> 00:15:35.260
So for example, App Engine--

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:37.690
very well integrated
with custom domains.

00:15:37.690 --> 00:15:40.330
You want to put your storefront
behind a domain that's

00:15:40.330 --> 00:15:41.680
yourstore.com.

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:44.860
Cloud Functions-- very good
integration with events.

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:47.350
You issue one command to deploy.

00:15:47.350 --> 00:15:49.734
It gets wired up to Pub/Sub.

00:15:49.734 --> 00:15:51.400
And now, all of a
sudden, you're already

00:15:51.400 --> 00:15:52.483
getting that event stream.

00:15:52.483 --> 00:15:54.880
You don't have to handle a
bunch of additional wiring.

00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:57.340
Tyler alluded to
automatic retries,

00:15:57.340 --> 00:15:59.740
and that's also a pretty
big feature for us.

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:02.544
So if you're getting
events, occasionally you're

00:16:02.544 --> 00:16:04.210
handling those events,
and your function

00:16:04.210 --> 00:16:05.986
might fail for whatever reason.

00:16:05.986 --> 00:16:08.110
Maybe you have a flaky
backend that that function's

00:16:08.110 --> 00:16:09.190
interacting with.

00:16:09.190 --> 00:16:12.220
You want to make sure that you
have a very easy way for you

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:14.920
to recover from those failures,
and you can potentially

00:16:14.920 --> 00:16:16.510
retry that event.

00:16:16.510 --> 00:16:18.790
Cloud Functions comes
with retries, sort of,

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:21.070
built in as a first-class
primitive because it's

00:16:21.070 --> 00:16:22.667
important to the
events use case.

00:16:22.667 --> 00:16:24.250
TYLER BUI-PALSULICH:
Another advantage

00:16:24.250 --> 00:16:27.460
is that with Cloud Functions,
these separate parts

00:16:27.460 --> 00:16:31.010
of your applications
can scale independently.

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:34.690
So if you have one
resource or endpoint

00:16:34.690 --> 00:16:37.150
that's being used
heavily by your users,

00:16:37.150 --> 00:16:43.390
or maybe you get a large amount
of events posted on Pub/Sub

00:16:43.390 --> 00:16:47.980
or files stored in Cloud
Storage, all of those functions

00:16:47.980 --> 00:16:52.010
can run independently from
the rest of your application.

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:55.120
So if you have resources that
your app uses on App Engine,

00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:57.370
you don't necessarily
need all of those

00:16:57.370 --> 00:16:58.830
to, say, resize an image.

00:16:58.830 --> 00:16:59.830
STEWART REICHLING: Yeah.

00:16:59.830 --> 00:17:02.800
And so I've seen customers
who actually end up

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:05.170
doing things like
deploying a function

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:07.750
and building all kinds of
complicated routing into it.

00:17:07.750 --> 00:17:10.119
And so their function
kind of grows

00:17:10.119 --> 00:17:14.030
into this really big thing that
can do many different things.

00:17:14.030 --> 00:17:16.150
But that's actually a
bit of an anti-pattern

00:17:16.150 --> 00:17:17.650
when it comes to functions.

00:17:17.650 --> 00:17:19.270
What we really
recommend is trying

00:17:19.270 --> 00:17:23.161
to keep your code isolated
to a specific use case,

00:17:23.161 --> 00:17:25.119
making sure that it does
one thing really well,

00:17:25.119 --> 00:17:27.130
because that
simplicity, actually,

00:17:27.130 --> 00:17:28.810
comes with a lot of benefits.

00:17:28.810 --> 00:17:30.520
You know, Tyler
mentioned the scaling.

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:33.040
Another one is, if you look
at the logs for your function,

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:36.170
you know exactly, for each
execution, for each function,

00:17:36.170 --> 00:17:38.780
what was happening if you're
printing stuff out to standard

00:17:38.780 --> 00:17:40.060
out as you're going.

00:17:40.060 --> 00:17:43.270
And so that simplicity
gives you a very easy way

00:17:43.270 --> 00:17:46.000
to reason about, where
are my failure points?

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:47.110
Where's my scaling?

00:17:47.110 --> 00:17:49.540
What's my usage
for specific tasks?

00:17:49.540 --> 00:17:52.540
That isolation, I think,
is a very big benefit

00:17:52.540 --> 00:17:53.470
of Cloud Functions.

00:17:53.470 --> 00:17:54.610
MARK MANDEL: That sounds great.

00:17:54.610 --> 00:17:55.985
Actually, speaking
of App Engine,

00:17:55.985 --> 00:17:57.910
and having done a lot
of work with App Engine,

00:17:57.910 --> 00:18:00.550
if I'm writing Go code in
App Engine, I write my code,

00:18:00.550 --> 00:18:02.500
and I run, like,
gcloud app deploy.

00:18:02.500 --> 00:18:04.750
And it bundles it all up,
and it sends it up somewhere

00:18:04.750 --> 00:18:05.867
to build it all.

00:18:05.867 --> 00:18:08.200
Is that experience similar
to what happens on Functions?

00:18:08.200 --> 00:18:10.000
Like, do you build the
actual thing for me?

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:12.310
Or do I have to build a
binary and ship it somewhere?

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:14.540
STEWART REICHLING: Yeah,
so we follow the same model

00:18:14.540 --> 00:18:15.340
as for App Engine.

00:18:15.340 --> 00:18:18.160
You give a source code,
we run that for you.

00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:20.770
You don't really have to
think about the build step.

00:18:20.770 --> 00:18:23.710
But since you're asking,
you gcloud functions

00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:26.470
deploy, pass a couple
command-line flags,

00:18:26.470 --> 00:18:30.190
everything in your directory
gets uploaded via that CLI,

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:33.580
and we, then, build
that function according

00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:34.940
to what you've given us.

00:18:34.940 --> 00:18:36.580
So if you've given
us a go.mod file,

00:18:36.580 --> 00:18:38.500
we're going to install
those dependencies.

00:18:38.500 --> 00:18:40.791
It's all going to be built
into a container that, then,

00:18:40.791 --> 00:18:42.040
runs on our infrastructure.

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:44.373
You don't, typically, have
to think about these details,

00:18:44.373 --> 00:18:45.840
but it's good to know.

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:47.380
If you give us a
vendor directory,

00:18:47.380 --> 00:18:49.550
we're going to
build that for you.

00:18:49.550 --> 00:18:52.990
And so we really want to handle
all kinds of different shapes

00:18:52.990 --> 00:18:55.942
and sizes of Go code that
people want to give us.

00:18:55.942 --> 00:18:58.150
And we want to make sure
that you don't actually have

00:18:58.150 --> 00:18:59.320
to think about the build step.

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:00.640
All of that is handled for you.

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:03.160
TYLER BUI-PALSULICH: The
two other common ways

00:19:03.160 --> 00:19:06.520
that you can deploy
Functions are with,

00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:08.800
one, in the Cloud Console.

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:10.750
So there is an
editor there which

00:19:10.750 --> 00:19:14.900
might work for very small
functions or limited changes.

00:19:14.900 --> 00:19:18.170
But it does work, and it's
nice for getting started.

00:19:18.170 --> 00:19:21.440
And then, the other option
is you can upload a zip file.

00:19:21.440 --> 00:19:24.250
So if, for whatever
reason, you would

00:19:24.250 --> 00:19:28.600
prefer not to deploy from, say,
the gcloud command-line tool,

00:19:28.600 --> 00:19:31.120
you can upload a zip file
and say, build the function

00:19:31.120 --> 00:19:31.915
from there.

00:19:31.915 --> 00:19:32.915
STEWART REICHLING: Yeah.

00:19:32.915 --> 00:19:34.900
And so the UI-based
use case, we actually

00:19:34.900 --> 00:19:37.750
see a lot of people using
that as their entry point

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:38.800
to the product.

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:42.640
You go to
console.google.com/functions.

00:19:42.640 --> 00:19:43.840
You click Create Function.

00:19:43.840 --> 00:19:45.190
You click Deploy.

00:19:45.190 --> 00:19:48.940
It's, literally, two clicks, and
you've got a function running.

00:19:48.940 --> 00:19:50.740
It's a great way
to get your first,

00:19:50.740 --> 00:19:52.580
sort of, step into the product.

00:19:52.580 --> 00:19:55.690
But once you graduate beyond
that, as Tyler mentioned,

00:19:55.690 --> 00:19:57.940
you can deploy with
the gcloud CLI.

00:19:57.940 --> 00:20:00.640
We also have an API that
you can deploy against.

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:04.015
You can also do integrations
like the zip file upload.

00:20:04.015 --> 00:20:06.190
MARK MANDEL: So one
thing I love asking you--

00:20:06.190 --> 00:20:07.910
I think this is going to be fun.

00:20:07.910 --> 00:20:11.050
So why would people
choose Go Cloud Functions

00:20:11.050 --> 00:20:13.924
over other languages in Cloud
Functions that we support?

00:20:13.924 --> 00:20:15.340
STEWART REICHLING:
Yeah, so that's

00:20:15.340 --> 00:20:17.920
a great question, and
a question that I also

00:20:17.920 --> 00:20:21.310
have to think about often,
which is why do we even

00:20:21.310 --> 00:20:22.630
support these new languages?

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:24.160
We already have Node.

00:20:24.160 --> 00:20:26.080
Can't everyone just write Node?

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:29.170
Well, the fact of the
matter is that not everybody

00:20:29.170 --> 00:20:30.400
wants to write Node.

00:20:30.400 --> 00:20:32.200
People want to write
in the language that's

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:33.730
most familiar to them.

00:20:33.730 --> 00:20:35.630
It makes it very
easy to get started.

00:20:35.630 --> 00:20:37.180
And alluding to
that earlier when

00:20:37.180 --> 00:20:38.980
I said we don't want
you to have to learn

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:40.420
a whole bunch of new stuff--

00:20:40.420 --> 00:20:42.850
well, if I tell you,
hey, use Cloud Functions,

00:20:42.850 --> 00:20:45.732
and before you do, learn Node,
that's a pretty big hurdle.

00:20:45.732 --> 00:20:47.815
MARK MANDEL: That's literally
what I went through.

00:20:47.815 --> 00:20:48.315
[LAUGHS]

00:20:48.315 --> 00:20:50.360
STEWART REICHLING: Yeah,
and so that's not great.

00:20:50.360 --> 00:20:51.480
I mean Node's a great language.

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:53.030
It's got a great
community around it.

00:20:53.030 --> 00:20:55.930
But we want to make sure that
this is as easy and intuitive

00:20:55.930 --> 00:20:57.350
to you as possible.

00:20:57.350 --> 00:20:59.980
The other thing that,
actually, I think,

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:01.150
there's that familiarity.

00:21:01.150 --> 00:21:03.700
The other piece is, just
as I mentioned earlier,

00:21:03.700 --> 00:21:05.380
picking the right
tool for the job--

00:21:05.380 --> 00:21:08.000
different languages have
different characteristics.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:09.580
So what we've seen
with Go is often

00:21:09.580 --> 00:21:12.150
you can find optimizations
in terms of speed,

00:21:12.150 --> 00:21:14.250
in terms of execution
performance.

00:21:14.250 --> 00:21:16.750
And so you might want to pick
the right language for the job

00:21:16.750 --> 00:21:20.290
based on its performance
characteristics, the ecosystem

00:21:20.290 --> 00:21:22.360
of packages that
exists around it,

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:24.970
the types of people that
work at your company

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:27.070
and their familiarity
and their desire

00:21:27.070 --> 00:21:28.240
to work with that language.

00:21:28.240 --> 00:21:30.634
MARK MANDEL: I was hoping
for way more flame war stuff,

00:21:30.634 --> 00:21:31.300
but that's cool.

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:31.780
That's good, too.

00:21:31.780 --> 00:21:32.946
STEWART REICHLING: [LAUGHS].

00:21:32.946 --> 00:21:34.420
AJA HAMMERLY: So
basically, you're

00:21:34.420 --> 00:21:36.580
trying to meet folks where they
are with the problems they have

00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:38.038
and the tools
they're familiar with

00:21:38.038 --> 00:21:40.330
and make it an easy
on-ramp for everyone.

00:21:40.330 --> 00:21:41.050
I approve.

00:21:41.050 --> 00:21:41.755
I think that's amazing.

00:21:41.755 --> 00:21:42.920
STEWART REICHLING:
Yeah, exactly.

00:21:42.920 --> 00:21:44.830
And I think that the
best thing we can do,

00:21:44.830 --> 00:21:47.830
then, is we come to you and
we say, hey, you write Go.

00:21:47.830 --> 00:21:49.780
Well, you can write
Go on this thing.

00:21:49.780 --> 00:21:52.420
And by the way, it
looks and feels like Go.

00:21:52.420 --> 00:21:53.170
It's idiomatic.

00:21:53.170 --> 00:21:54.586
TYLER BUI-PALSULICH:
Yeah, I think

00:21:54.586 --> 00:21:56.650
that's the same
principle that goes into,

00:21:56.650 --> 00:22:01.960
let's say, for HTTP functions,
where it's just a HandlerFunc.

00:22:01.960 --> 00:22:04.540
And it's the same
guiding principle

00:22:04.540 --> 00:22:09.160
for, you shouldn't need to
learn Cloud Functions Go.

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:11.670
It's just you know
Go, or you like Go,

00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:14.380
or you're learning Go,
or none of the above.

00:22:14.380 --> 00:22:17.910
And you shouldn't have to do
something special just for this

00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:18.910
STEWART REICHLING: Yeah.

00:22:18.910 --> 00:22:20.860
And just to throw a
little teaser out there,

00:22:20.860 --> 00:22:23.561
because I know some people
aren't as interested in Go

00:22:23.561 --> 00:22:25.060
as they might be
in other languages,

00:22:25.060 --> 00:22:27.040
we're working on other
language, as well.

00:22:27.040 --> 00:22:30.070
You should expect to see more
language support in the future.

00:22:30.070 --> 00:22:33.069
Google Cloud supports a
broad range of languages,

00:22:33.069 --> 00:22:34.360
and we want to conform to that.

00:22:34.360 --> 00:22:36.190
AJA HAMMERLY: So
that's a lovely segue

00:22:36.190 --> 00:22:39.460
into asking, so what comes
next for Go Cloud Functions?

00:22:39.460 --> 00:22:42.550
So Go 1.11 is in beta right now.

00:22:42.550 --> 00:22:44.050
That means we still
have polish work

00:22:44.050 --> 00:22:48.640
to do, both on the reliability
side, on the performance side.

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:50.450
Faster deployment
times, for example,

00:22:50.450 --> 00:22:53.290
is one of the pieces of
feedback we've received.

00:22:53.290 --> 00:22:55.570
We want to give
better error messages.

00:22:55.570 --> 00:22:57.490
One of the nice
things about being

00:22:57.490 --> 00:22:59.410
in a serverless
environment is that you

00:22:59.410 --> 00:23:01.581
don't have to think about
the infrastructure that's

00:23:01.581 --> 00:23:02.080
underneath.

00:23:02.080 --> 00:23:04.960
But one of the downsides
is that you can't really

00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:07.240
just SSH into a
machine and necessarily

00:23:07.240 --> 00:23:08.484
figure out what's going on.

00:23:08.484 --> 00:23:10.150
And because you don't
have that control,

00:23:10.150 --> 00:23:12.790
giving back meaningful
error messages

00:23:12.790 --> 00:23:16.120
is super important, because the
worst thing is something breaks

00:23:16.120 --> 00:23:18.430
and you have no way of
figuring out what happened.

00:23:18.430 --> 00:23:20.814
So for us, there's a lot
of work on the polish side.

00:23:20.814 --> 00:23:22.480
TYLER BUI-PALSULICH:
With serverless, it

00:23:22.480 --> 00:23:25.220
is a bit of a black box
of what that machine is,

00:23:25.220 --> 00:23:26.930
and you don't know
what's happening.

00:23:26.930 --> 00:23:28.930
And you make whatever
API calls, and you don't

00:23:28.930 --> 00:23:30.410
know how long they're taking.

00:23:30.410 --> 00:23:33.430
And so a good way to debug
that, and a general, just,

00:23:33.430 --> 00:23:37.030
development piece of advice,
is to use tracing and logging,

00:23:37.030 --> 00:23:38.000
in general.

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:40.390
And so tracing,
as a quick primer,

00:23:40.390 --> 00:23:44.920
is you can trace the function
calls and API calls that you're

00:23:44.920 --> 00:23:49.990
making from your code and
say this Translation API took

00:23:49.990 --> 00:23:53.410
150 milliseconds to
compute, or to respond,

00:23:53.410 --> 00:23:57.790
so it's not worth really looking
into this if my user code only

00:23:57.790 --> 00:23:59.230
added 10 milliseconds to this.

00:23:59.230 --> 00:24:00.787
There isn't much
optimization to add.

00:24:00.787 --> 00:24:02.620
MARK MANDEL: And there's
a library for that.

00:24:02.620 --> 00:24:02.960
Right?

00:24:02.960 --> 00:24:04.043
TYLER BUI-PALSULICH: Yeah.

00:24:04.043 --> 00:24:05.920
So there's something
called OpenCensus which

00:24:05.920 --> 00:24:08.390
is an open source library.

00:24:08.390 --> 00:24:11.710
It's mainly sponsored
by Google and Microsoft,

00:24:11.710 --> 00:24:16.150
where you can export to
whatever tracing, visualization,

00:24:16.150 --> 00:24:18.770
and debugging provider
that you prefer.

00:24:18.770 --> 00:24:20.590
So for Google
that's Stackdriver,

00:24:20.590 --> 00:24:22.712
but there are many
other offerings

00:24:22.712 --> 00:24:23.670
that do the same thing.

00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:27.050
MARK MANDEL: And we even have
a podcast on it-- episode 118.

00:24:27.050 --> 00:24:29.905
STEWART REICHLING: Nice.

00:24:29.905 --> 00:24:32.030
MARK MANDEL: Favorite
question I also like to ask--

00:24:32.030 --> 00:24:33.404
what's the most
interesting thing

00:24:33.404 --> 00:24:35.310
you've seen so far people
build with Go Cloud

00:24:35.310 --> 00:24:38.190
Functions-- or wacky,
or just plain strange?

00:24:38.190 --> 00:24:41.760
STEWART REICHLING: So there's
a very interesting company

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:44.130
out of Europe in
the farming space

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:46.320
that I spoke with recently.

00:24:46.320 --> 00:24:47.700
And they do
something really cool

00:24:47.700 --> 00:24:51.540
where they have a bunch of
different IoT devices sitting

00:24:51.540 --> 00:24:55.980
in their farms, each with
variable degrees of high speed

00:24:55.980 --> 00:24:56.760
internet.

00:24:56.760 --> 00:25:01.110
And those are emitting events
about the state of the farms--

00:25:01.110 --> 00:25:03.990
so temperature
readings, humidity.

00:25:03.990 --> 00:25:07.650
And all of that data gets
fed to a message bus which

00:25:07.650 --> 00:25:10.470
feeds into Pub/Sub
and then triggers

00:25:10.470 --> 00:25:13.590
Cloud Functions that run in
response to those readings.

00:25:13.590 --> 00:25:16.530
And so Cloud Functions is
a very useful piece of glue

00:25:16.530 --> 00:25:19.500
there, where you can take
that data, put it somewhere

00:25:19.500 --> 00:25:21.270
where it's more
persistent, and now you

00:25:21.270 --> 00:25:25.260
have a pretty reliable way
of seeing, in one state, OK,

00:25:25.260 --> 00:25:26.880
what is the state of my farm?

00:25:26.880 --> 00:25:28.810
What does everything look like?

00:25:28.810 --> 00:25:31.170
And so you get Cloud
Functions as glue code,

00:25:31.170 --> 00:25:33.390
but also handling
data and making

00:25:33.390 --> 00:25:36.720
those use cases very easy
through very simple bits

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:37.574
of code.

00:25:37.574 --> 00:25:39.990
You know, you're talking about
less than 100 lines of code

00:25:39.990 --> 00:25:41.280
to set this whole thing up.

00:25:41.280 --> 00:25:42.420
MARK MANDEL: Tyler, you
got anything, as well?

00:25:42.420 --> 00:25:44.628
TYLER BUI-PALSULICH: I don't
think anything specific.

00:25:44.628 --> 00:25:48.960
But just commenting on
Pub/Sub and just the things

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:53.910
that people can push through
it and the amount of execution

00:25:53.910 --> 00:25:56.490
that can go through it and
be triggered and scaled

00:25:56.490 --> 00:26:00.330
automatically with, as
Stewart said, very little code

00:26:00.330 --> 00:26:01.860
that you've actually written--

00:26:01.860 --> 00:26:03.700
it's just really cool to see.

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:04.950
STEWART REICHLING: Yeah,
it's pretty mind-blowing

00:26:04.950 --> 00:26:06.720
when you see the
types of things people

00:26:06.720 --> 00:26:10.350
are able to build when you give
them these simple primitives

00:26:10.350 --> 00:26:13.740
and good integrations, things
like, hey, every time something

00:26:13.740 --> 00:26:17.940
happens to my Firestore, I want
to run some code in response

00:26:17.940 --> 00:26:18.617
to that.

00:26:18.617 --> 00:26:20.700
People can come up with
some pretty amazing things

00:26:20.700 --> 00:26:22.470
when you just give
them those pieces.

00:26:22.470 --> 00:26:25.110
Hey, can I shout out some more
stuff that's coming up for Go?

00:26:25.110 --> 00:26:29.070
We're working on Go 1.12,
so expect that sometime

00:26:29.070 --> 00:26:30.000
in the near future.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:32.100
It'll be pretty
similar to Go 1.11.

00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:33.540
There will be some improvements.

00:26:33.540 --> 00:26:35.331
We're putting in all
this work to make sure

00:26:35.331 --> 00:26:37.530
that we are respecting
the language communities

00:26:37.530 --> 00:26:39.030
and really supporting
the language.

00:26:39.030 --> 00:26:40.946
We want to make sure
that we also stay current

00:26:40.946 --> 00:26:42.090
with the language versions.

00:26:42.090 --> 00:26:43.500
MARK MANDEL: So that
sounds really great.

00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:45.090
If people are like,
this sounds awesome,

00:26:45.090 --> 00:26:47.340
and they want to learn stuff
about Go Cloud Functions,

00:26:47.340 --> 00:26:48.450
where should people go?

00:26:48.450 --> 00:26:50.550
Are there some good resources
that people can take advantage

00:26:50.550 --> 00:26:50.790
of?

00:26:50.790 --> 00:26:52.123
STEWART REICHLING: Three things.

00:26:52.123 --> 00:26:55.350
The first is we had
a pretty cool launch

00:26:55.350 --> 00:26:57.120
video for this runtime.

00:26:57.120 --> 00:26:58.770
You can see it on
YouTube right now.

00:26:58.770 --> 00:27:00.927
Search Go Cloud Functions,
should pop right up.

00:27:00.927 --> 00:27:02.760
MARK MANDEL: We'll put
it in the links, too.

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:05.051
STEWART REICHLING: It walks
you through the basic types

00:27:05.051 --> 00:27:07.500
of functions, how to
deploy, what the experience

00:27:07.500 --> 00:27:09.050
looks like from gcloud.

00:27:09.050 --> 00:27:10.300
It's about five minutes long.

00:27:10.300 --> 00:27:11.920
It's a great way to get started.

00:27:11.920 --> 00:27:13.800
I'd also recommend
the quickstarts.

00:27:13.800 --> 00:27:15.570
So go to the docs page.

00:27:15.570 --> 00:27:17.010
We'll have a link to that below.

00:27:17.010 --> 00:27:19.440
That will, basically,
walk you through-- if I'm

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:21.870
a good developer, how
do I go from having

00:27:21.870 --> 00:27:26.040
no code to using gcloud to
deploying my first Go function?

00:27:26.040 --> 00:27:28.500
And the last thing
I'd shout out is

00:27:28.500 --> 00:27:30.060
if you only have a
couple of minutes,

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:31.900
the quickest way
to get started--

00:27:31.900 --> 00:27:33.450
go to the Cloud Console.

00:27:33.450 --> 00:27:35.080
Go to the Functions page.

00:27:35.080 --> 00:27:36.220
Click Create Function.

00:27:36.220 --> 00:27:37.320
Select the Go runtime.

00:27:37.320 --> 00:27:39.900
It will give you a
pre-populated snippet of code

00:27:39.900 --> 00:27:41.010
that you can deploy.

00:27:41.010 --> 00:27:41.577
Click Deploy.

00:27:41.577 --> 00:27:42.660
You're going to get a url.

00:27:42.660 --> 00:27:44.040
You can invoke it.

00:27:44.040 --> 00:27:46.200
I'd say that's the easiest
way to go from nothing

00:27:46.200 --> 00:27:48.840
to actually trying the
product out, having

00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:51.285
your first experience with
it, in less than five minutes.

00:27:51.285 --> 00:27:52.260
MARK MANDEL: Fantastic.

00:27:52.260 --> 00:27:54.570
Well, Tyler,
Stewart, thank you so

00:27:54.570 --> 00:27:56.190
much for joining
us on the podcast

00:27:56.190 --> 00:27:57.900
today and telling us all
about Go Cloud Functions.

00:27:57.900 --> 00:27:58.830
STEWART REICHLING: Yeah.

00:27:58.830 --> 00:27:59.670
Thanks for having us.

00:27:59.670 --> 00:28:00.753
TYLER BUI-PALSULICH: Yeah.

00:28:00.753 --> 00:28:01.335
Thank you.

00:28:01.335 --> 00:28:03.060
AJA HAMMERLY: So thanks
again to Stewart and Tyler

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:05.190
for joining us on the
podcast today and teaching us

00:28:05.190 --> 00:28:06.390
all about Go Cloud Functions.

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:08.000
There's a lot of exciting
stuff going on there.

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:08.730
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:28:08.730 --> 00:28:10.720
So we have a question of
the week, as per always.

00:28:10.720 --> 00:28:12.570
And I get to ask you a
question of the week.

00:28:12.570 --> 00:28:13.440
Hahaha.

00:28:13.440 --> 00:28:15.540
So the question of the
week is how many ways

00:28:15.540 --> 00:28:17.940
can you run containers
on Google Cloud Platform?

00:28:17.940 --> 00:28:19.690
AJA HAMMERLY: I believe
the answer is 4.

00:28:19.690 --> 00:28:20.190
[DING]

00:28:20.190 --> 00:28:20.870
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:28:20.870 --> 00:28:22.530
AJA HAMMERLY: This is
something that I discovered

00:28:22.530 --> 00:28:24.270
a couple of years
ago, and I have

00:28:24.270 --> 00:28:26.620
found that most people
actually haven't figured out.

00:28:26.620 --> 00:28:29.230
Everyone knows about Kubernetes
because Kubernetes is awesome.

00:28:29.230 --> 00:28:29.585
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:28:29.585 --> 00:28:30.630
AJA HAMMERLY: A fair
number of people

00:28:30.630 --> 00:28:31.920
know about App Engine Flexible--

00:28:31.920 --> 00:28:32.370
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:28:32.370 --> 00:28:33.660
AJA HAMMERLY: --where
you can run containers,

00:28:33.660 --> 00:28:35.224
which is how you
can run languages

00:28:35.224 --> 00:28:36.390
that we don't support fully.

00:28:36.390 --> 00:28:40.800
But maybe you really want to run
a COBOL container or something

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:42.400
for your application.

00:28:42.400 --> 00:28:43.572
So that's one and two.

00:28:43.572 --> 00:28:45.030
And what a lot of
people don't know

00:28:45.030 --> 00:28:47.155
is that you can actually
run containers straight up

00:28:47.155 --> 00:28:48.420
on Google Compute Engine.

00:28:48.420 --> 00:28:50.760
So you can run them either
as part of a managed instance

00:28:50.760 --> 00:28:52.350
group, or you can
actually just deploy

00:28:52.350 --> 00:28:54.054
a VM based on a container image.

00:28:54.054 --> 00:28:55.470
It's really handy
if you just need

00:28:55.470 --> 00:28:57.370
to spin up a single
instance of a container.

00:28:57.370 --> 00:28:58.800
And we're going to
have a link in the show

00:28:58.800 --> 00:29:00.990
notes to more information
about this on a document that's

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:03.615
actually been updated relatively
recently because this is still

00:29:03.615 --> 00:29:05.460
a really common use
case that people have.

00:29:05.460 --> 00:29:08.260
And if you don't need all of
the really cool orchestration

00:29:08.260 --> 00:29:11.050
abilities and discoverability
abilities of Kubernetes,

00:29:11.050 --> 00:29:12.840
this is another way
to run containers that

00:29:12.840 --> 00:29:14.430
is absolutely 100% supported.

00:29:14.430 --> 00:29:14.720
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:29:14.720 --> 00:29:16.386
AJA HAMMERLY: The
other super cool thing

00:29:16.386 --> 00:29:18.849
is the managed instance groups
support both health tracking

00:29:18.849 --> 00:29:19.515
and autoscaling.

00:29:19.515 --> 00:29:20.355
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:29:20.355 --> 00:29:22.470
AJA HAMMERLY: So you
get some of the benefits

00:29:22.470 --> 00:29:24.670
of an orchestration tool
but not all of them.

00:29:24.670 --> 00:29:25.920
But it's a lot lighter weight.

